{
    "duration": 242.35000610351562,
    "language": "english",
    "text": "It's a firefly night. Emma couldn't sleep. Outside her window, tiny lights blinked on and off in the summer darkness. Mommy, what are those little lights? she asked, pointing out the window. Those are fireflies, sweetheart, her mother said with a smile. Would you like to go see them? Emma nodded eagerly. Her little brother Liam was already fast asleep, but Nom promised they could take him tomorrow night. They tiptoed downstairs and out the back door. The warm evening air wrapped around them like a blanket. The grass felt cool beneath Emma's bare feet. Fireflies are special insects, Mom explained. They make their own light. It's called bioluminescence. Bioluminescence, Emma repeated carefully, loving the way the big word felt in her mouth. All around them, tiny lights winked on and off. It reminded Emma of the stars, except these stars danced and moved through the air. Can we catch one? Emma whispered. Mom nodded. Just for a moment, to look at it closely. Then we have to let it go. She showed Emma how to cup her hands gently. Together, they waited patiently until a firefly drifted near. With a swift but gentle movement, Mom caught one and carefully transferred it to Emma's waiting hands. Emma peeked through a tiny gap between her thumbs. Inside, the firefly crawled across her palm. Then, suddenly, its lower body lit up with a magical greenish-yellow glow. It tickles, Emma giggled. Why does it light up, Mommy? That's how fireflies talk to each other, Mom explained. The males flash their lights in a special pattern, and the females answer back. It's how they find each other. Emma watched in wonder as the firefly blinked again. I think it's saying hello to me. Maybe it is, Mom said. Now, let's say goodbye and let it go find its friends. Emma opened her hands. The firefly paused for a moment on her fingertip, glowed once more, and then flew away to join the others. The next evening, Emma could hardly wait for the sun to set. This time, Liam came too, his eyes wide with excitement. Look, Liam, Emma pointed as the first fireflies began to appear. They're nature's night Dad brought out a jar with holes punched in the lid. We can catch a few to look at them more closely, but remember... We have to let them go, Emma finished. She was already an expert. Together, the family caught several fireflies in the jar. In the soft glow of the porch light, they watched the insects crawl along the glass, lighting up every few seconds. They're like tiny stars we can hold, Liam whispered in awe. When it was time, Emma helped Liam unscrew the lid. They watched as the fireflies spiraled upward, blinking their farewell messages as they disappeared into the night. That night, tucked in bed, Emma whispered to her mother. Can we have a firefly night again tomorrow? Mom smiled. All summer long, sweetheart, the fireflies will be waiting for us. Emma closed her eyes, dreaming of dancing lights and the magical creatures that carried summer's glow in their tiny bodies.",
